## Native model orchestration

Outside the shared Grok effort and review rules, choose the agent prompt, model, and
reasoning effort independently. Do not map models permanently to roles such as
scout, architect, or coder.

`pi-subagents` accepts a per-call `model` override. Pass the full selector with
an effort suffix when useful, for example
`openai-codex/gpt-5.5:high` or `anthropic/claude-fable-5:medium`.

Available managed pool:

| Model | Selector | Start | Cost | Intelligence | Taste | Vision |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
| GPT-5.5 | `openai-codex/gpt-5.5` | high | — | — | — | yes |
| Fable 5 | `anthropic/claude-fable-5` | high | 2 | 9 | 9 | yes |
| Opus 4.8 | `anthropic/claude-opus-4-8` | xhigh | 4 | 7 | 8 | yes |
| Sonnet 4.6 | `anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-6` | high | — | — | — | yes |
| GLM-5.2 | `ollama/glm-5.2:cloud` | provider default | 7 | 8 | 7 | no |

The `Start` column is only an initial reasoning-effort prior. Opus 4.8 is the
exception and always runs at xhigh effort. Adjust other models for the task's
uncertainty and validation surface. The comparative ratings are selection
context, not permanent role assignments. Pi's installed catalog does not
currently expose GPT-5.6 or native xAI OAuth; OMP has the expanded pool.

Prefer Pi-native subagents over launching `claude`, `codex`, `grok`, or
`ollama` CLIs from inside Pi. Keep the CLIs for provider-native features or
when operating from another harness.

The main model owns scope, model selection, synthesis, validation, and the
final answer. Use parallel subagents only for genuinely independent work.

